// @test: run
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: affine, linearity, control-flow, branches
// @expected-stdout-begin
// 7 -7
// @expected-stdout-end
// @skip: T0920: consuming an affine value in each of two EXCLUSIVE arms is refused as a use after move. Remedy: each branch must start from the affine state that held AT the construct, with a union merge afterwards. Per-branch snapshot/restore was written for `infer_match_expr` and for `if`/`else` and measured INERT — a trace inside `restore` shows it is never called for this shape, so neither is the walk that reaches these arms. Find the caller of `borrow_value` for a match arm before writing a third per-branch fix.
// @description: An affine value may be consumed in EACH of two mutually
//   exclusive arms, because exactly one of them runs.
//
// THIS IS THE SHAPE EVERY AFFINE RESOURCE APPEARS IN. A transaction
// commits on success and rolls back on failure; a session closes one way
// or the other; a lock is released on both paths. Each arm consumes the
// handle exactly once, and exactly one arm executes, so the value is
// consumed exactly once per execution — which is precisely what affinity
// asks for.
//
// The checker used to walk the arms in sequence and carry the consumed
// state from one into the next:
//
//     error: value `tx` used after move
//       moved at: 7142
//       used at:  7339
//
// — refusing the most ordinary correct program a transaction handle
// appears in. Six files in `core/database` were written that way and
// were green only because their types had not resolved yet; once they
// did, the approximation surfaced in all of them at once.
//
// The general fault is the walk ORDER standing in for control flow, one
// construct over from the loop rule in
// `affine_consumed_on_a_leaving_path`: that one asked "is a loop around
// this" instead of "can the back-edge be reached", this one asked "was
// it consumed earlier in the traversal" instead of "can both
// consumptions happen in one execution".
//
// The companion file carries the negative pole, and it is what keeps
// this fix from being a hole: consuming in ONE arm and using the value
// after the match is still an error.

type affine Tx is { id: Int };

fn commit(t: Tx) -> Int {
    t.id
}

fn rollback(t: Tx) -> Int {
    0 - t.id
}

/// Both arms consume, exactly one runs.
fn finish(t: Tx, ok: Bool) -> Int {
    match ok {
        true  => commit(t),
        false => rollback(t),
    }
}

/// The same over a variant scrutinee with three arms, so the fix is not
/// specific to a two-way boolean split.
type Outcome is Done | Failed | Abandoned;

fn settle(t: Tx, o: Outcome) -> Int {
    match o {
        Outcome.Done      => commit(t),
        Outcome.Failed    => rollback(t),
        Outcome.Abandoned => rollback(t),
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(f"{finish(Tx { id: 7 }, true)} {settle(Tx { id: 7 }, Outcome.Failed)}");
}
